```{r}
library(tidyverse)
library(fpp3)

retail_guess <- read_csv("data/retail_sales_raw.csv")

retail_raw <- read_csv("data/retail_sales_raw.csv", na = ".",
  col_types = cols(observation_date = col_date(format = "%Y-%m-%d"),
  RSAFS = col_double(),
  RSAFSNA = col_double()))

retail_raw_long <- retail_raw |>
  select(observation_date, RSAFS, RSAFSNA) |>
  pivot_longer(cols = c(RSAFS, RSAFSNA), names_to = "series_id", values_to = "sales")

retail_ts <- retail_raw_long |>
  mutate(Month = yearmonth(observation_date)) |>
  as_tsibble(index = Month, key = series_id)

can_gas <- canadian_gas |>
  select(Month, Volume)
```

## Time and Tsibbles

The first dataset is U.S. retail and food services sales from FRED, which comes from the Census Bureau. It has two monthly series in millions of dollars, RSAFS (seasonally adjusted) and RSAFSNA (not seasonally adjusted), running from `r min(retail_ts$Month)` to `r max(retail_ts$Month)`. In `retail_ts` the index is `Month` and the key is `series_id` since there are two series stacked in long format. The second dataset is `canadian_gas` from the fpp3 package, which is monthly natural gas production in Canada in billions of cubic meters from 1960 Jan to 2005 Feb. Its index is also `Month` but it has no key because there's only one series.

When I first read in the csv without specifying types, `observation_date` came in as `` `r class(retail_guess$observation_date)` ``. I re-read it with `col_types` anyway so the date and the two sales columns are set explicitly, and `na = "."` handles FRED's missing values.

```{r}
head(retail_guess)
head(retail_raw)
```

Wide vs long:

```{r}
head(retail_raw)
head(retail_raw_long)
```

The tsibble:

```{r}
head(retail_ts)
has_gaps(retail_ts)
```

The header shows `[1M]` so it picked up the monthly frequency, and `has_gaps()` is FALSE for both series so there are no missing months.

